## 2026-06-26 (fix/substring-protection-with-digits)
- Fixed false-positive masking of legitimate words sitting next to digits.
  - "hello9" was being cleaned to "****o9" because the Scunthorpe-style
    substring guard (`_is_pure_alpha_substring`) only applied when the entire
    surrounding `\w`-context was pure alphabetic. A trailing/leading digit made
    the context non-alpha ("hello9"), disabling the guard so "hell" matched.
  - Added `_alpha_word_context()` which expands across ASCII letters only, and
    used it to derive the word passed to the substring guard. Now "hello9",
    "9hello", "shell9", "scunthorpe9", etc. stay clean, while bare profanities
    with appended digits ("hell9", "ass9") are still masked.
  - Added regression test `test_alpha_substring_protection_survives_adjacent_digits`.

